00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
it is wednesday 6 41  time for warren cup of joe and metro  sheriff joe lombardo is joining us live  today from metro headquarters good  morning sheriff it's good to see you  alex good to see you we want to start  with this today sheriff the murder trial  of former minneapolis police officer  derek shovin  in the death of george floyd it started  last week so when that verdict does come  down no matter what happens  there is a strong chance for  demonstrations nationwide  is metro preparing for protests here  locally  yeah i would say that we are i mean  fingers crossed like everybody else  um but i think the important piece on  this is  to be proactive and i believe we have at  metro been proactive in this and and  that's  communication with the community  excuse me i think it's very important to  talk about this right not  keep our fingers crossed and hope it  doesn't happen i i  in traditional police work we prepare  for  the inevitable and you know we uh we  over  commit and then hopefully you can just  de-escalate  if it doesn't turn out the way is is  expected  in this case there could be a plethora  of happenings or decisions you know  acquittal an indictment  dismissal you know even if there is an  indictment  people may not be happy with the level  of indictment so that's the important  thing to consider  so what we've done here at metro we  proactively met with the community  uh on several occasions prior to today  and we hope for the best moving forward  and what i mean by hope for the best we  hope for the best that  the community is uh holding hands and  they're together and they're talking  about it versus  uh causing destruction as a result of it  we have pretty good history here in  community interaction and community  engagement  and if you saw last week we did the  metro multicultural advisory committee  press conference and that was a press  conference  orchestrated by them not me not the  government per se  and reaching out to the community and  say hey have patience  we've had success here in the past with  controversial issues  because we've had that patient and we  relied on metro's response  right now sheriff state lawmakers are  considering several bills  that would make it more difficult for  police to use deadly force  including when dispersing protests what  are your thoughts on that  well i think it's it's something that  has constantly be looked at  um you know referenced the on the heels  of  george floyd in the incident there in  milwaukee or minneapolis the uh  we constantly have to talk about it and  here's the reason why  the use of deadly force is a very big  issue it's a very important issue and  it's important that the police  departments  explore the sanctity of life associated  with the use of deadly force  and and and what i'm proud of  here at lbmpd is we've been very  proactive in that space  and it isn't because you know we're the  best of the best i even though i agree  with that  and i would say that but it's because we  had warts  upon us in the past and we realized what  was  occurring in the deadly force base and  then we had to do something about it so  we've been very proactive in placing  policies and  training and procedures in place to um  make sure that we're utilizing deadly  force appropriately  and not just because we can um and  i think we're progressive in that space  and so if you look at the legislature  back to your original question  they're addressing those issues but  those issues  are more in line with law enforcement  statewide versus lb and pd a lot of the  bills have been adopted adapted  and modified to match our policy here at  lbmpd  to make it a statewide function versus  an individual function  and you know and then the important  piece is the duty to render aid the duty  to intervene  those are the key components associated  with  george floyd's incident and those of uh  issues that we've addressed previously  yeah and it certainly has become a  larger conversation since  george floyd's death last year and it  should be so  our last question here today sheriff  we've talked about a spike in car thefts  and break-ins the past few months  do you know what's behind this increase  does metro see a trend there  uh well that's that's a great question  and to answer your question  uh no and but we could surmise in a  couple of different ways um yes there  has been a significant increase 25  percent  across the board in vehicle thefts uh  lower a lot lower than that in actual uh  auto burglaries around two percent  increase so when i say 25 percent that's  a big number because  on an annual basis we average around 7  000 uh vehicle thefts a year  uh so if you put that in and you know  the the math associated with that that's  a big deal  and uh so we're addressing it we're  looking at it and i think part of it is  because people are out and about more  vehicles are being utilized more  a reference post covered and this  new phenomenon that's going on with  catalytic converters and they're in the  rare  earths that's are located in those  converters uh  and how much they cost now per pound in  years past  so there's a you know they may be  stealing the car to  eventually obtain the catalytic  converter versus crawling under it and  stealing it  at the location where it's parked so we  haven't been able to put our finger on  it yet but we're paying attention  and i think the important piece on this  is to educate the public make them aware  this is occurring  and be more vigilant associated with  target hardening  uh their individual vehicles i mean just  this morning on my way here  i pull in the convenience store to get a  cup of joe  and and there was an individual in the  store left their car running out in  front of the store  you know so they could run and run out  but that's what people look for  that's they sit in parking lots and pay  attention to that  or if you leave your keys in your car in  front of your house or in your garage  those are the kind of things that you  need to be aware of to make it harder  and more difficult for a crook to do  what they do yes and don't leave the car  running  all right sheriff lombardo it's great to  see you thank you so much sir have a  great morning  good morning thank you alex all right if  you have a question for sheriff jill and  barter you can send it to us on our  website 8newsnow.com  morning cup of joe is every wednesday  right here on good day las vegas

